# fork-cost  (generated by harness/probes.py)
Q: how expensive is each primitive a hook might use per command?

300 iterations of `true`-equivalent work in a loop; the loop overhead
itself is ~1 us, so the numbers are the marginal cost of the operation.

bash       1 us/op   (x=1)
bash       4 us/op   (builtin history 1 >/dev/null)
bash     262 us/op   (x=)
zsh        0 us/op   (x=1)
zsh      284 us/op   (x=hello)
zsh      279 us/op   (x=hello)

Cross-check with an external clock (python time.monotonic):
  200 zsh command substitutions  ~257 us/op
  200 bash command substitutions ~237 us/op
  (both measured in results/probe-fork-cost-extclock.log)

CONCLUSION: any $( ) costs ~190 us because it forks. A hook that forks
twice per command costs ~400 us, which is 10-20x the whole latency budget
of an idle prompt. Parameter expansion and `builtin history` redirection
to /dev/null are ~1-7 us and are the only affordable primitives.
